// Undertaking Hooks (D20), p.76-77. "The Last Vinyard" is as printed.
(
    name: "Undertaking Hook",
    die: D20,
    entries: [
        (1, 1, (title: "The Silent Village", text: "You stumble upon a small, isolated village that is eerily untouched. There are no Penitents, no signs of the Flesh, and the people go about their business in absolute silence. They communicate with gestures and written notes. Is this a sanctuary, or is their silence a different kind of damnation?")),
        (2, 2, (title: "The Weeping Penitent", text: "A lone Penitent has been seen weeping tears of blood and scraping crude symbols into the dirt. It doesn't attack, but its sorrowful song causes nosebleeds and despair in those who hear it. What is it trying to communicate? Can it be saved?")),
        (3, 3, (title: "The Cannibals", text: "A community of survivors has taken to ritual cannibalism. They claim eating the flesh of those who listened to the Choir grants them temporary immunity to its song. They offer you a place at their fire, but the next \"meal\" is a captured traveler who looks suspiciously healthy.")),
        (4, 4, (title: "The Last Vinyard", text: "An old winemaker fanatically protects his vineyard, the last in the region. He claims his wine (called \"Tears of Adam\") strengthens one's will against the Choir. He needs help defending his land from an encroaching Flesh-blight, but his methods of \"fertilizing\" his vines are deeply disturbing.")),
        (5, 5, (title: "The Bloody Tithe", text: "The fortified town of Provins offers shelter and trade, but the price of entry is a \"flesh tithe\": a finger, an ear, a pound of flesh, given to a silent, pulsating mound at the town's center. They claim this placates the Flesh, but the mound has started to grow.")),
        (6, 6, (title: "The Lost Child", text: "A child has gone missing from a survivor camp. Their mother gives you a crude drawing the child made: a picture of themselves walking towards a \"singing flower\" in the nearby woods. She begs you to find them before the Choir does.")),
        (7, 7, (title: "The Map", text: "You find the corpse of a Flesh Apostate, their body a ruin of half-formed tumors. Clutched in their hand is a map tattooed onto a strip of human skin. It supposedly leads to a \"deaf spot\" where the Choir's song cannot reach, deep within a Penitent crusade's marching route.")),
        (8, 8, (title: "The Dead River", text: "The local river has turned thick and grey, choking the land, and it's not the work of the Flesh. Upstream, a Choir legion and a Flesh infestation fought to a standstill, their remains creating a new, hybrid horror that now pollutes the water with its very existence.")),
        (9, 9, (title: "The Collector", text: "A paranoid noble, barricaded in his manor, wants to hire you. He \"collects\" unique specimens of the new world and desires a \"perfectly silent\" stone from a Flesh-eaten quarry. The last group he sent to retrieve one never returned.")),
        (10, 10, (title: "The Black Bell", text: "You hear a rumor of a bell forged from a fallen star. It produces no sound, only a wave of pure silence that can supposedly shatter a Penitent's body and repel the Flesh. It was last seen being transported to a monastery that is now the heart of a Choir nesting ground.")),
        (11, 11, (title: "The Silent Affliction", text: "A new affliction is spreading through a refugee camp. Victims' tongues swell and split, forming new mouths that babble discordant notes. You must find the source (a tainted well, a Holy object, a hidden Penitent) before the whole camp is lost to the Choir's song.")),
        (12, 12, (title: "The Rescue", text: "A half-built stone bridge is the only way across a chasm now filled with writhing tendrils of Flesh. Unfortunately, the local master mason was captured by a Choir procession. His apprentice begs you to rescue him, as only he knows the final steps to securing the keystone.")),
        (13, 13, (title: "The Last Book", text: "A dying scholar offers you his most prized possession, an uncorrupted relic, in exchange for one last book. The book, a treatise on acoustics, is in a library that is being consumed by the Flesh. The walls have turned to sinew, and the books are being absorbed into the organism.")),
        (14, 14, (title: "The True Rapture", text: "A charismatic prophet preaches that the true Rapture is at hand. He's gathering followers to march on a \"sacred site\" where they will be saved. His path leads directly into the heart of wartorn territory. Is he a charlatan, a madman, or something worse?")),
        (15, 15, (title: "The Seed", text: "You find a small, pulsating, fleshy orb. A dying Apostate whispers it is a \"seed of the Flesh\". If planted in fertile ground (or a living creature), it will grow into a massive infestation. Do you destroy it, or could it be used as a weapon?")),
        (16, 16, (title: "The Messenger", text: "A rider, his body a horrific miscreation of human and angel, collapses before you. He presses a sealed message into your hands, begging you to deliver it to the \"Lord of the Sunken Keep\". His last act is to point a trembling, claw-like finger down a forgotten road.")),
        (17, 17, (title: "The Monk's Chant", text: "In a ruined cathedral, what is left of an order of monks still perform their daily chants. Their clean song clashes with the Choir's hymn, creating a zone of relative safety. But a new, aggressive Penitent has begun to hunt them, and their sanctuary is fading.")),
        (18, 18, (title: "The Broken Truce", text: "A community of Unbroken has a fragile truce with a pack of Flesh-beasts. The beasts keep the Choir away, and the Unbroken leave offerings of livestock for them. The truce is shattered when the beasts' alpha is found murdered, and the pack wants vengeance.")),
        (19, 19, (title: "The Sunken Temple", text: "An earthquake has revealed the steeple of a temple that was swallowed by the earth centuries ago. Locals whisper that it was a place of a forgotten, silent pagan god. It might hold answers or a respite from the Choir, but the shifting earth is unstable, and the Flesh is drawn to the disturbance.")),
        (20, 20, (title: "The Face in the Flesh", text: "A massive, ever-expanding mound of Flesh has begun to form a face: a gargantuan, vaguely human visage that stares blankly at the sky. It doesn't seem hostile, but its growth is displacing a nearby survivor camp. What does it want? What happens when it finally finishes forming?")),
    ],
)
